      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τὰς ἐνίων μηνύειν κοινωνίας",
          "english_quote": "and disclose some of his unions",
          "issue": "The note treats ἐνίων as partitive without adequately identifying its referent. It is masculine or neuter genitive plural, not the feminine form expected for a straightforward partitive referring to κοινωνίαι. The construction can denote unions of some gods or persons, or unions with some partners. The draft's wording can be defended contextually through the latter interpretation, but its note neither explains that construction nor preserves the alternative scope.",
          "suggested_revision": "Correct the note to distinguish these interpretations. If ἐνίων denotes the persons whose unions are narrated, consider 'I shall begin with Zeus himself ... in disclosing the unions of some of them.' If retaining 'some of his unions,' explain it as a contextual rendering of unions with some partners and preserve the alternative 'the unions of some [gods/persons].' Review whichever revised wording and note are selected.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὁπότε ἔμελλεν γαμεῖν καὶ ὡς ἐπὶ πρώτῃ εὐνῇ κατὰ τοὺς πολλοὺς μοιχὸς εἰδὼς οὐ γινόμενος",
          "english_quote": "when he was about to marry, he knew that although most people would regard him as an adulterer at the first bedding, he was not becoming one",
          "issue": "The draft introduces a positive popular judgment and an opposing concession: most people supposedly regard Zeus as an adulterer, although he knows otherwise. The supplied construction instead places μοιχός with the negated γινόμενος, with κατὰ τοὺς πολλούς qualifying the judgment that he was not becoming an adulterer. Merely retaining 'not' later in the English does not preserve this scope. The note does not establish a defensible Greek construction for the added positive judgment.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'when he was about to marry, knowing that, since this was a first bedding, he was not becoming an adulterer in most people's judgment'. Explain the compressed participial construction and disclose that ὡς ἐπὶ πρώτῃ εὐνῇ can also be represented more cautiously as 'as at a first bedding.' Remove the unsupported 'although most people would regard him as an adulterer.' The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὁμοιωθεὶς κόκκυγος πτερῷ",
          "english_quote": "after being made like a cuckoo in plumage",
          "issue": "The supplied comparison is to κόκκυγος πτερῷ, a cuckoo's wing or feather. The main text changes the comparison into likeness to the whole cuckoo with respect to its plumage. Although the note acknowledges the literal image, the main wording does not retain it transparently.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'after being made like a cuckoo's wing' or 'after being made like a cuckoo's feather,' preserving the other lexical possibility in the note. Do not replace the supplied comparison with a more familiar whole-bird transformation.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "5.13",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
