      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀπὸ τῆς Κρόνου ἐκτομῆς",
          "english_quote": "as a result of the cutting performed by Cronus",
          "issue": "In this birth-from-Uranus-and-Thalassa context, ἐκτομή carries the image of cutting off or castration, which generic 'cutting' weakens. Taking Κρόνου subjectively, as the agent, is contextually defensible, but the note presents that interpretation as established and dismisses the objective interpretation, castration undergone by Cronus, without disclosing the grammatical ambiguity.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'as a result of the castration performed by Cronus.' Explain that the Greek literally attributes the cutting off or castration to Cronus, that the surrounding birth imagery supports reading him as its agent, and that the genitive can alternatively designate the person undergoing it. Do not certify the subjective interpretation as uniquely supplied by the wording.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "Καλλιστοῖ τῇ Λυκάονος ἠγριώθη λέων, καὶ ἄλλον τίκτει Ἀρκάδα.",
          "english_quote": "For Callisto daughter of Lycaon he became a fierce lion, and she bears another Arcas.",
          "issue": "The subject of τίκτει is implicit. Continuing Zeus as subject gives 'he begets,' while a contextual shift to Callisto supports 'she bears.' Also, ἄλλον can present Arcas as another offspring in the catalogue rather than necessarily another person bearing the same name. The draft selects both a female subject and 'another Arcas' without disclosing these material alternatives.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'and she bears another child, Arcas,' with a note preserving 'he begets another, Arcas' as a subject alternative and explaining the possible more literal reading 'another Arcas.' Identify 'child' as an explanatory English expression of the offspring context, not restored Greek. The existing female-subject interpretation need not be rejected merely because an alternative exists, but it requires honest disclosure.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
